## Proof

Let $\pi:\widetilde{X}\to X$ be the universal cover. Since $\widetilde{X}$ has finitely many sheets, $\pi_1(X)\cong\operatorname{Deck}(\widetilde{X}/X)$ is a **finite** group.

Choose a lift $\widetilde{f}:\widetilde{X}\to\widetilde{X}$ of $f\circ\pi:\widetilde{X}\to X$ (this lift exists because $\widetilde{X}$ is simply connected, hence lifts of any map into $X$ exist). By construction
$$\pi\circ\widetilde{f}=f\circ\pi.$$

**Step 1. $\widetilde{f}^{\,2}$ is a deck transformation.**

Using the lifting relation twice and the fact that $f$ is an involution ($f\circ f=\mathrm{id}_X$):
$$\pi\circ\widetilde{f}^{\,2}=f\circ\pi\circ\widetilde{f}=f\circ(f\circ\pi)=(f\circ f)\circ\pi=\mathrm{id}_X\circ\pi=\pi.$$

A self-map of $\widetilde{X}$ that preserves every fiber of $\pi$ is exactly a deck transformation. Hence
$$\widetilde{f}^{\,2}\in\operatorname{Deck}(\widetilde{X}/X)\cong\pi_1(X).$$

**Step 2. $\widetilde{f}^{\,2}$ has finite order.**

Since $\pi_1(X)$ is finite, every deck transformation has finite order. Let $k=|\,\widetilde{f}^{\,2}\,|$ be the order of $\widetilde{f}^{\,2}$ in the deck group, so $1\le k<\infty$ and $(\widetilde{f}^{\,2})^{k}=\mathrm{id}_{\widetilde{X}}$.

**Step 3. $\widetilde{f}$ has finite period.**

Since $\widetilde{f}$ commutes with $\widetilde{f}^{\,2}$ (powers of the same map),
$$\widetilde{f}^{\,2k}=\bigl(\widetilde{f}^{\,2}\bigr)^{k}=\mathrm{id}_{\widetilde{X}}.$$

Thus $\widetilde{f}$ has finite period, dividing $2k$.

**Conclusion.** Yes. The lift $\widetilde{f}$ has finite period; explicitly, if $k$ is the order of the deck transformation $\widetilde{f}^{\,2}$ in the finite group $\operatorname{Deck}(\widetilde{X}/X)\cong\pi_1(X)$, then $\widetilde{f}^{\,2k}=\mathrm{id}$.

